nd salt  there's a lot of evidence from sugary  drinks that it'll that they'll  reformulate rather than pass on the cost  from mexico from hungary so we are  confident that this is a tax that will  take out the worst make the worst foods  uh a bit better for people it's not  enough on its own but it is  uh it is it is necessary to make the  transformation  yeah i i mean presumably the other  aspect to it is people  preparing their own low fat low sugar  low salt meals to go along with not not  eating the junk food but  you know that does require time and in a  society such as ours how likely is it  that people  getting home after a long day at work  are going to spend a half hour the hour  of the hour and a half it might take to  prepare that meal  rather than just reaching into the  freezer  yeah well i think you're right it does  take time and it takes skills  and it's a long term transition we've  put in place a recommendation for a huge  education package  on uh cookery and nutrition because  we've lost the skills through three  generations  and that's why you know that is going to  take time we eat more processed food  than any other country in europe about  50  compared to 14 in italy and we need to  shift that and eat more unprocessed food  cook more fruit and veg  but we can't wait for that transition  which is why we need to make the  processed food the worst food  better for us the the strategy mr  dimbley was widely trained  skills skills sorry to interrupt sorry  turns over the  the the um the uh the strategy as  advertised as pre-built  certainly people were expecting to see  quite a lot in in about  reducing the amount of of meat that we  eat i was speaking with someone from the  national farmers union just a little bit  earlier on today  they made the point that actually what  you're suggesting is less of the junk  meat rather than you know the steaks and  the beefy bakes that i'm particularly  fond of  yeah there is there is about six  chapters  of of 16 on meat it's a very very  complicated  topic and uh not all meat is equal we  need to reduce our meat eating by about  30  we cannot meet our carbon targets or our  nature targets  unless we do that it just takes up too  much land but there is a way of doing  that by cutting out the process stuff  and and keeping the roast chicken the  steak the things that we really enjoy so  i think there's a way through on meat  that actually  we as a country can navigate and still  have  the kind of landscapes that we enjoy so  much that make up such a big part of our  kind of cultural heritage i i suppose  the key question then mr tumblebee is  what is the likelihood of the government  listening to you in all of this  introducing a sugar tax a  assault tax reducing a reliance on junk  food  when you have in boris johnson a  populist in number 10.  well i think boris johnson's changed  right so uh  his experience during covid has  fundamentally changed the way he thinks  about  the food system and our need to do  something about it but kind of to answer  the question more broadly whenever you  do i've done  three nav independent reviews and  whenever you do one  uh you know that your your work is not  guaranteed to be taken up and so you do  everything you can to  communicate it in a simple way to bring  coalitions around the recommendations  and in the last two reviews that i've  done almost everything's been  uh implemented and i will be doing my  utmost to make the case  uh for the government to implement these  things which are doable  practical and we think will make a a  real transformation of the food system

